# studies-archive-absorption-siren

**A SIREN as a fittable 3-D absorption field.** Two versions of the same idea, from two branches:

**The student project** (`student/AbsorptionSIREN` @ `97c9076a`, June 2026) fits a SIREN to a 3-D
absorption field in a cylinder (R = 16.9, H = 38) and checks recovery against a known truth field. It
is standalone — a replica of the `SIREN_Absorption.ipynb` notebook rather than library code — with its
own loader, model and trainer, a 3-D visualiser, and eight 778 KB `.npz` parameter sets: the fitted
fields, the injector fields, and `true_siren_params.npz`, the truth it is scored against.

**The merged-quality twin** (`unification-siren` @ `1b206d16`) is the same capability written as
library code: `lucid/simulation/fields.py`'s `make_field` returns `(apply_fn, init_params)` and is
called once at setup outside JIT, the SIREN representation reuses the shared `lucid.siren.core.SIREN`
(`fields.py:62`, `:176`), and `gauge=True` pins the field's spatial-deviation mean to 0 — which is
what removes the field-against-`L_abs` degeneracy and is required for a joint global+field
calibration. It is coherent and tested — `tests/test_absorption_field.py` and `test_absorption_field_e2e.py` — on its branch, and on
no other ref.

## Do not delete `square_output` from the SIREN model

`unification-siren:lucid/simulation/fields.py:62` (inside `_make_siren`, `:173-244`) is the **only**
consumer anywhere of `SIREN(square_output=False)`. The flag itself is
`lucid/siren/core.py:68` on `main` (the plan's target tree calls that file `lucid/siren/model.py`).
That one call site is why the flag survives the cleanup even though nothing on `main` sets it: a dead-flag sweep that removes it
silently breaks this branch's only route back into the tree, and the breakage would not show up in any
test on `main`. If `square_output` ever does go, it goes together with a decision about this
capability — not before it.

## The branch is retained

`student/AbsorptionSIREN` is **not** deleted and **not** rewritten without the owner's sign-off
(`D-HIST-6`). The student rebases their two unique library commits onto the rewritten base as
`format-patch` output; until that has happened, the branch and this tarball are both load-bearing.
This asset exists so the work is recoverable, not so the branch can go.

